(* rss.ebnf — RSS 2.0 as a schema grammar over the XML element vocabulary.

   RSS 2.0 is a dialect of XML, so it is NOT a character grammar like
   xml.ebnf: lang/xml.ebnf already parses the bytes into the generic Tag
   tree. This grammar describes the RSS *vocabulary* — which elements
   nest in which, and which carry attributes — so the projector
   (service/rss.go) can walk a parsed Tag tree and restructure it into the
   typed rss.proto AST. It is the EBNF analogue of a DTD/XSD, compiled to
   proto by lang/cmd/genproto_rss through the same gluon engine that turns
   dtd.ebnf into dtd.proto.

   Conventions (interpreted by genproto_rss / service.CompileGrammar):

     - A rule is an element. Its body lists the child elements (as
       references to other rules) and its attributes.
     - An attribute is written `at_<name>` — a reference that has no rule
       of its own. It lowers to a plain `string <name>` field (the `at_`
       marker is stripped). The marker is required because an attribute and
       an element can share a name: `<url>` is a child element of <image>,
       but url= is an attribute of <enclosure>/<source>.
     - `text` is the character content of a leaf element; it also has no
       rule and lowers to a `string text` field.
     - Any reference with no rule therefore becomes a string field; every
       other reference becomes a message field. (Same rule CompileDTD uses:
       declared -> message, undeclared -> scalar.)

   What is NOT here (the projection schema doesn't carry it — enforced in
   service/rss.go. These are regular or context-free, NOT CFG-inexpressible:
   they are out of the grammar because attributes/leaves are opaque strings and
   projection checks neither values nor cardinality):
     - version must be "2.0" (a fixed attribute literal — regular); an <item>
       needs at least a title or description (bounded presence — context-free);
       <image> width <= 144 and height <= 400 (finite numeric bounds — regular);
       <guid> isPermaLink defaults to "true" (a default value, not a membership
       constraint at all); link/url URI schemes; RFC-822 dates (regular).

   Namespace extensibility (RSS 2.0: "may contain elements and attributes
   not described here, only if defined in a namespace") is enforced by the
   projector, not the grammar: a namespace-qualified element/attribute is a
   tolerated extension, an unprefixed unknown one is invalid.

   The start rule (rss) is first. *)

rss = at_version , channel ;

channel = { title | link | description | language | copyright
          | managingEditor | webMaster | pubDate | lastBuildDate
          | generator | docs | cloud | ttl | image | rating
          | textInput | skipHours | skipDays | category | item } ;

item = { title | link | description | author | category | comments
       | enclosure | guid | pubDate | source } ;

image = { url | title | link | width | height | description } ;

textInput = title , description , name , link ;

cloud     = at_domain , at_port , at_path , at_registerProcedure , at_protocol ;
enclosure = at_url , at_length , at_type ;
source    = at_url , text ;
category  = at_domain , text ;
guid      = at_isPermaLink , text ;

skipHours = { hour } ;
skipDays  = { day } ;

title          = text ;
link           = text ;
description    = text ;
language       = text ;
copyright      = text ;
managingEditor = text ;
webMaster      = text ;
pubDate        = text ;
lastBuildDate  = text ;
generator      = text ;
docs           = text ;
ttl            = text ;
rating         = text ;
url            = text ;
width          = text ;
height         = text ;
name           = text ;
author         = text ;
comments       = text ;
hour           = text ;
day            = text ;
